    "summary": "Dark Star shines, tight The Eleven, vintage R&B‑country blend",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a standout Dark Star and a virtuoso performance of The Eleven. Tom Constanten’s surprise Bach minuet adds quirky charm between Mama Tried and Yellow Dog Story. Overall the band is relaxed yet inventive, with solid rhythm support from Phil, Bill and Mickey.",
    "review": "On December 11, 1969 at the Thelma Theater the Dead offered a time‑capsule set that swings from straight‑ahead R&B to psychedelic exploration. The first half builds through familiar covers – Casey Jones, Hard To Handle and Mama Tried – before launching into an expansive Dark Star that feels both relaxed and inventive, with Jerry’s fluid lead weaving around Phil’s tight bass lines. A highlight is the seamless segue into Saint Stephen, then The Eleven where Phil’s virtuosity shines and Bill and Mickey lock in on a tricky drum break after the “coral sands below” vocal. Tom Constanten pops up for an unexpected Bach minuet that adds a quirky classical flavor, while Pigpen trades harmonica licks with Jerry on Next Time You See Me. The second set keeps the momentum with heartfelt renditions of Morning Dew and the upbeat Sittin’ On Top Of The World, closing out with the classic sandwich of Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Cosmic Charlie. While the official soundboard source is marred by low fidelity and digital glitches, several excellent mono audience recordings capture the warm ambience and preserve the musical magic, making this a must‑listen for fans of early Dead improvisation.",
